WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.032 --> 00:00:15.864
The dwikkoji that gives your back view an elegant touch — its design and color decide the overall impression.

2
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.448
Dwikkoji generally fall into two styles.

3
00:00:20.784 --> 00:00:34.440
First, traditional Asian designs using materials like jade, mother-of-pearl, or cloisonné create a refined, dignified image, and pair best with a classic, neatly finished updo.

4
00:00:34.776 --> 00:00:49.128
Second, Western-style designs in cubic zirconia, pearl, or metal add glamour and a modern touch, sparkling best against a chic wavy style or a fusion hanbok.

5
00:00:49.464 --> 00:00:55.296
The key to choosing well is matching the color of your jeogori.

6
00:00:55.632 --> 00:01:04.992
Pair a pink jeogori with pink-toned ornaments and a blue jeogori with blue-toned ones for a cohesive, polished look.

7
00:01:05.328 --> 00:01:12.648
An ornament that clashes with the jeogori's color can actually look tacky, so be careful.

8
00:01:12.984 --> 00:01:20.448
In particular, the cheap white plastic pieces some hanbok shops throw in for free not only lower the sense of refinement,

9
00:01:20.784 --> 00:01:28.560
but traditionally can also bring mourning to mind, so it's best avoided on a happy occasion.

10
00:01:28.896 --> 00:01:32.664
Size matters too.

11
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:43.872
An ornament that's too large will overshadow the hairstyle and hurt the overall finish, so choose a size that acts as an accent, matched to your head shape and hair volume.

12
00:01:44.208 --> 00:01:53.496
If you discuss your desired image — understated or glamorous — with an artist at a one-on-one prior consultation and try it on in person,

13
00:01:53.832 --> 00:02:01.128
you'll look flawless and confident from every angle guests see you from on the wedding day.
